  "documentation": {
    "range": "This species' distribution is currently understood to include the Mediterranean and Black Sea basins, including the Sea of Azov, as well as the northeastern Atlantic coast northwards from the Strait of Gibraltar to the southern English Channel (but see 'Taxonomic Notes'). In the Black Sea basin it is expanding into major rivers, and landlocked subpopulations have been recorded hundreds of kilometres upstream in the Danube, Southern Bug, Dnieper, Don and Kuban systems. It has also been introduced to the Volga River system in Russia, where it now occurs upstream to Moscow.",
    "population": "This species' overall population trend has not been quantified. Many subpopulations remain locally abundant, but ongoing anthropogenic degradation of coastal wetlands and seagrass beds is estimated to be driving a general population decline in the Mediterranean basin. Conversely, Black Sea subpopulations appear to be increasing via expansion into major tributary river systems.",
    "habitats": "This species is euryhaline, inhabiting the lower reaches of rivers and  their estuaries, coastal lagoons, inshore marine zones and artificial habitats such as drainage channels and salt exploitation sites. It is particularly abundant in shallow seagrass meadows but is also found in open habitats dominated by detritus, mud or sand. Some studies suggest that its upstream expansion into major  rivers of the Black Sea basin has been facilitated by  dam construction and transportation in ship ballast-water. It is a carnivorous species preying largely on zooplankton, sometimes including its own juveniles. <br/><br/>Like some other members of the family Syngnathidae, this species exhibits highly-specialised role-reversal during the reproductive cycle. Females are larger and more competitive than males, and possess ornamentation designed to attract partners. In contrast, males are responsible for brood care and provide nutrients to the developing embryos at an energy investment cost believed to exceed that of most other vertebrates. This species is also polygynandrous, with both sexes participating in multiple mating events over the course of a single reproductive season. Males possess an inverted marsupium (brood pouch) located on the underside of the tail. Females deposit eggs into the marsupium, where they are fertilised and incubated for about 20-30 days, depending on water temperature. Individual males may receive eggs from multiple females during the reproductive season, and these are segregated by maternity within the marsupium. Newborn juveniles are immediately benthic, with no pelagic phase, and this potentially contributes to limited connectivity between individual subpopulations. <br/><br/>Maximum lifespan is thought to be around 4 years, with sexual maturity attained at 1 year of age and reproduction occurring from spring to summer. Maximum recorded total length is 21 cm.",
    "threats": "Some subpopulations are threatened by pollution from a variety of sources, or drainage of coastal wetlands for agricultural, industrial, touristic or urban development. For example, since 2016 the Mar Menor coastal lagoon in Murcia, southeastern Spain, has suffered a rapid ecological collapse. Despite being protected at the national and international scales, eutrophication driven by decades of agricultural pollution has resulted in the disappearance of extensive sea grass meadows and the fish communities associated with them. Elsewhere, adult spinal deformities linked to domestic and industrial pollution have been observed in the Lake of Tunis, Tunisia, since the mid-2000s.",
    "measures": "This species occurs in a number of officially-protected areas, but there are no specific conservation measures in place. Further research focussed on its population and habitat trends is required, particularly in the Mediterranean basin.",
    "use_trade": "This species is not used or traded, but is sometimes harvested as bycatch in commercial fisheries.",
    "rationale": "The Black-striped Pipefish remains widespread and abundant at many locations within its range, and its non-native distribution is expanding. It is plausible that anthropogenic degradation of coastal environments is driving subpopulation declines in some areas, particularly the Mediterranean basin, and this warrants further study given its uncertain taxonomic status. It is currently assessed as Least Concern.",
    "trend_justification": null,
    "taxonomic_notes": "This species' taxonomy remains partially unresolved, with molecular studies demonstrating the current concept to comprise at least three potentially distinct taxa in the western Mediterranean alone (Sanna <em>et al</em>. 2013).<br/><br/>Some authors refer to the subpopulation inhabiting the Azov and Black Sea basins, including their affluent rivers, as <em>S. nigrolineatus</em> Eichwald 1831, but this taxon is currently treated as a synonym of <em>S. abaster</em>. The former subpopulation from the Caspian Sea basin is currently valid as <em>S. caspius</em> Eichwald 1831 (Fricke <em>et al</em>. 2023)."
  },
